<!--
  /data-binding — bind:value / bind:checked (two-way), on: events, bind:this refs.
  Binding connects the DOM to signals; live server data lives on /data.
-->
<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Core concepts</p>
  <h1>Data binding</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Binding connects the DOM to your signals — an input's value, a checkbox's
    state, an event, an element reference. Each is a single directive, and each
    reads or writes the same signals the rest of your view uses.
  </p>

  <h2>Two-way binding</h2>
  <p>
    <code>bind:value</code> keeps a text input and a signal in sync in both
    directions: type in the field and the signal updates; set the signal and the
    field follows. <code>bind:checked</code> does the same for a checkbox.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.twoway}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  <p>
    <code>on:click</code>, <code>on:input</code>, <code>on:submit</code> — the
    <code>on:</code> prefix attaches a handler to any DOM event. Handlers are
    where you write signals; the template's read side stays a pure function of
    state.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.events}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Refs</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes you need the element itself — to focus it, measure it, or hand it
    to a library. <code>ref()</code> in the controller plus
    <code>bind:this</code> in the template gives you a handle you read like a
    signal.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.refs}></sui-codeblock>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Reads and writes stay separate.</strong> The template reads signals;
    handlers write them. Two-way <code>bind:</code> is the one place both happen
    at once — and it's exactly sugar for that read plus an <code>on:input</code>
    write.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/control-flow">← Control flow</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/data">Live data →</a>
  </div>
</div>
